okay today I'm going to talk to you  about quadrats what quadrat is is it's a  square frame enclosing a known area so  for example a lot of quadrats are one  meter squared now what a quadrat could  be useful is they could be used to help  estimate the number of organisms in a  particular area and I'm going to show  you how to do that so what I've drawn  here at the bottom what this represents  is it's a bird's eye view of a field  containing lots and lots of plants  called poppies um now if this was a real  field obviously it would be nearly  impossible to count each poppy  individually to figure out how many  poppies are in the whole field so what  we can do is we can use a quadrat to  help estimate how to do that so the  first thing you do and this is the  really important word that we always  need to think about when we think about  quadrats is we randomly place the  quadrat down so we randomly do this so  let's say it randomly falls here so this  here just represents the quadrat that  I've put it down okay now if I put the  quadrat down we should see that there'd  be lots of poppies underneath the  quadrat in the holes of the Quadra so  the quadrat just allows it to be nice  and easy to count those poppy so one two  three four five six seven eight nine ten  eleven twelve thirteen fourteen fifty  sixty seven eight nine twenty one two  three four - five - six - seven - eight  - nine thirty so because of the squares  it just makes it easy to count the  number of poppies within that particular  quadrat so the next step will be this  you're after you've randomly placed the  quadrat then you count the organisms now  quadrats are usually used to count  plants but they can be used to count  other things such as slugs or other  other maybe insects that are less mobile  if it's a butterfly think it wouldn't be  as good because the butterflies could  just fly away so they use the counting  organisms now what you need to do next  obviously  this one space in the field isn't  necessarily representative of the whole  of the field for example you can see  there's a high density of poppies here  so as a result we need to repeat process  for a total of 10 times so we randomly  going to place the quadrat nine more  times across the field so let's say this  one there so it's 2 3 4 5 6 7 8 9 10 so  as a result we're getting a kind of  we're getting a view of the poppy  distribution across the whole of the  field rather than just one particular  section so what we do next is we find  the total number of poppies so let's  imagine the total number of poppies that  we counted in our quadrat is 200 poppies  now our next step is we want to find the  mean number of poppies so the average  number per quadrat now to do that in  this particular example we'd have to do  200 because that's how many poppies  they're wearing total divided by the  number of quadrats so in this particular  example it would be 200 divided by 10  which would be 20 poppies now the next  step is what we have to do is obviously  we need to find the area of the whole  field so we'd have to measure the length  of this side the field let's say that's  50 meters in length and we'd have to  measure the length of this side of the  field let's say that's 40 meters in  length right there so the next step is  to find the total area of the whole  field so the total area of the field is  going to be 50 times 40 which equals  2,000 meters squared right we've just  got one more step to do we know that the  average number of poppies per  meter squared is 20 so in 2,000 meters  squared all we're going to have to do is  20 poppy's times 2,000 because that's  how many meter squares we've got  effectively so the answer is 40,000  Papi's is a estimate of how many Papi's  are in the whole field so just to  quickly remind you a quadrat is just a  square frame to help us count organisms  it could be used if we place it randomly  across an area of a field ten times it  can help us estimate the number of  organisms in the entire field you count  the organisms you find the total you  then find the mean per quadrat then what  you need to do is times it by the area  of the whole field and we should have an  estimate of how many poppies are in the  whole field without having to count each  one individually thank you
 

